data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"That's right. The stretch factor at $-1$ is $-2$, so the nudge is doubled and reversed. One note: your thinking field has said \"inside first, then outside\" on every answer, including this one, where it doesn't apply. I'll assume it's filled in automatically and not ask about it again. That means the wrong options have to do the diagnosing, so I'll write them carefully.\n\n## A composition is a pipeline\n\nThe second foundation is about structure, not calculus. Write $h(x) = f(g(x))$ as a pipeline:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n  X[\"x\"] --> G[\"g\"] --> U[\"u = g(x)\"] --> F[\"f\"] --> Y[\"y = f(u)\"]\n```\n\n$f$ never receives $x$. It only receives $u$, whatever $g$ hands it. So:\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> The **only** way $x$ can affect $y$ is by changing $u$. Two inputs that give the same $u$ give the same $y$, whatever $f$ is.\n\nThis holds for every composition, because \"$f$ applied to $g(x)$\" means exactly this. Applied to nudges: nudging $x$ affects $y$ only through the nudge it causes in $u$. Whatever $f$ does, it does to $\\Delta u$, never to $\\Delta x$ directly.\n\nThat's also why $f'$ is evaluated at $g(x)$, which you already had. $f$ is working at the point $u$, so its stretch factor at $u$ is the one that applies."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01YFfDRfQzoQC7Z7cLrfHYTt","name":"quiz","arguments":{"type":"check","nodeId":"pipeline","question":"Let $h(x) = f(g(x))$. At $x = a$, $g'(a) = 0$, and $f$ is extremely steep at $g(a)$: $f'(g(a)) = 1000$. You nudge $x$ by a tiny $\\Delta x$. To first order, what happens to $h$?","options":[{"label":"It changes by about $1000\\,\\Delta x$, because $f$ is steep there","value":"steep"},{"label":"It changes by about $1000"}]},"partialJson":"{\"type\": \"check\", \"nodeId\": \"pipeline\", \"question\": \"Let $h(x) = f(g(x))$. At $x = a$, $g'(a) = 0$, and $f$ is extremely s