ue":"zero"},{"label":"It can't be determined without knowing $f'(a)$","value":"fa"}],"explanation":"$f$ only responds to changes in $u$. Since $g'(a)=0$, a tiny $\\Delta x$ produces $\\Delta u \\approx 0\\cdot\\Delta x = 0$ to first order. A stretch factor of $1000$ applied to a nudge of (essentially) zero is still (essentially) zero:\n$$\\Delta h \\approx 1000\\cdot\\Delta u \\approx 1000\\cdot 0 = 0$$\n- \"About $1000\\,\\Delta x$\" treats $f$ as if it received $\\Delta x$ directly, skipping the pipeline.\n- \"$"},"partialJson":"{\"type\": \"check\", \"nodeId\": \"pipeline\", \"question\": \"Let $h(x) = f(g(x))$. At $x = a$, $g'(a) = 0$, and $f$ is extremely steep at $g(a)$: $f'(g(a)) = 1000$. You nudge $x$ by a tiny $\\\\Delta x$. To first order, what happens to $h$?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"It changes by about $1000\\\\,\\\\Delta x$, because $f$ is steep there\",\"value\":\"steep\"},{\"label\":\"It changes by about $1000\\\\,\\\\Delta x$ plus a small correction from $g$\",\"value\":\"sum\"},{\"label\":\"It barely changes, because $u$ barely moves, so $f$ has nothing to stretch\",\"value\":\"zero\"},{\"label\":\"It can't be determined without knowing $f'(a)$\",\"value\":\"fa\"}], \"explanation\": \"$f$ only responds to changes in $u$. Since $g'(a)=0$, a tiny $\\\\Delta x$ produces $\\\\Delta u \\\\approx 0\\\\cdot\\\\Delta x = 0$ to first order. A stretch factor of $1000$ applied to a nudge of (essentially) zero is still (essentially) zero:\\n$$\\\\Delta h \\\\approx 1000\\\\cdot\\\\Delta u \\\\approx 1000\\\\cdot 0 = 0$$\\n- \\\"About $1000\\\\,\\\\Delta x$\\\" treats $f$ as if it received $\\\\Delta x$ directly, skipping the pipeline.\\n- \\\"$"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":3,"cacheRead":17510,"cacheWrite":17302,"totalTokens":34817,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":17302},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792861034148,"responseId"